*What improved.* The trained loop copied the correct literal key in the first executed table lookup on 25/32 questions versus 9/32 for Base. Both chose the correct search column on 14/32. The returned column was correct on 12/32 versus 11/32. For example, the loop looked up the winning car Wolf WR3 and returned the driver, Dave Kennedy. On the recorded package inventories the loop grounded two messages against one. No package difference satisfied the full two-message endpoint. The loop completed 67/96 user requests versus 54/96, while its table completions fell to 14/32 versus 16/32.

*What the paired reasoning test found.* Twenty-one of the 24 recurrent sentence pairs had precisely one correct member. Only the pair about a short story and a canceled class yielded both correct answers for the loop. Base alone solved the pair about who had lost or inherited money. For the primary meaning-and-table endpoint there were two loop-only complete pairs and one Base-only complete pair (two-sided exact $p=1$). Completing more ordinary questions, or selecting a more plausible name on one variant, did not establish reliable sensitivity to the change in meaning.

*Independent reading.* We read every non-exact, completed table reply with model identities hidden and the question and publisher answer visible. Of 23 distinct replies requiring review, one gave the unambiguous reference and one contained a conflicting numerical claim. The latter correctly associated 9.7 million BlackBerry devices with 10.4 million Symbian devices, then claimed the same 9.7 million for every one million. Counting it as ambiguous gives seven grounded loop answers versus one Base answer. Crediting its first statement recovers the registered seven versus two. Both readings give one complete table pair for the loop and zero for Base. The reviewer was the project coding assistant. External human adjudication would provide an additional check.

*Where training effort went.* Of 163,358 token targets, 118,351 came from table calls, 12,552 from returned table answers, 14,423 from meaning questions and 18,032 from rehearsal. The optimizer sampled 734 of 768 meaning turns and 374 of 384 table calls. Among the 192 table pairs, the first different parameter was the search column 139 times, the literal key 48 times and the return column five times. Later fields also received token loss. Their identities seldom received the extra paired-margin term.

*Active recurrence.* On a separately fixed 381-token file question, setting the trained second-pass contribution to zero changed full-vocabulary next-token logits by RMS 0.9549, maximum 3.75. Restoring 0.35 reproduced every active logit. The most likely token stayed the same. Both independent physical cache offsets reached 381 and all seven projection objects were shared. The paired reasoning, pooled, family retention, first-token and throughput gates failed. The fitted model’s matched-depth, 128K and deployment follow-ups closed under the registration.
